course: everyday
lesson: 73
title: Brushing your teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After you eat, tiny germs in your mouth feed on bits of food and make a sticky layer called plaque; the plaque makes acid that slowly eats holes in your teeth.
explain: Brushing twice a day, after breakfast and last thing before bed, clears the plaque away; the night brush matters most, because while you sleep there is less spit to wash and protect your teeth.
explain: Toothpaste has fluoride, which makes teeth stronger: spit the toothpaste out, but do not rinse with lots of water, so a little fluoride stays on the teeth.
explain: Brush gently in small circles with a soft brush for about two minutes, and do the tongue too; a toothbrush is personal and is never shared.
explain: A boy in Toronto, tired after a long day, still brushes for two minutes at bedtime, because he knows night is when teeth are most at risk.
fact: Germs and food make plaque, a sticky layer that causes holes in the teeth.
fact: Teeth are brushed twice a day, after breakfast and last thing before bed, for about two minutes.
fact: At night there is less spit in the mouth to protect the teeth, so the bedtime brush is the most important.
fact: After brushing you spit out the toothpaste but do not rinse straight away, so the fluoride keeps protecting the teeth.
fact: Teeth are brushed gently in small circles with a soft brush, because hard scrubbing hurts the gums and wears the teeth.
fact: Germs on the tongue cause bad breath, so the tongue is brushed gently too.
fact: Gums that bleed a little usually have plaque on them; gentle brushing helps them heal, and you tell a parent if it goes on.
fact: A toothbrush with bent, splayed bristles does not clean well and is changed about every three months.
fact: A toothbrush is never shared, because it can carry germs and blood from one mouth to another.
fact: Toothpaste is spat out, not swallowed, because too much swallowed fluoride is not good for the body.
fact: Acidic drinks like orange juice soften the tooth enamel for a while, so you brush before breakfast or wait about an hour after it before brushing.
exercise: When should you brush your teeth? | After breakfast and last thing before bed | Only in the morning, just before you go to school | Only when your teeth feel dirty | Once a week on Sunday evening
exercise: Why is the bedtime brush so important? | At night there is less spit to protect the teeth | Teeth grow most while you sleep | Toothpaste only works in the dark | Germs sleep at night, so they are easy to brush away then
exercise: What should you do after brushing? | Spit out the toothpaste but not rinse straight away | Rinse your mouth out many times with lots of fresh water | Swallow the foam that is left | Drink a glass of juice to wash it away
exercise: Why is it good not to rinse with water straight after brushing? | The fluoride stays on the teeth and keeps protecting them | Water makes the teeth turn yellow | Rinsing straight after brushing wastes far too much water | Water washes the enamel off the teeth
exercise: How should you brush your teeth? | Gently in small circles with a soft brush | As hard as you can with a stiff, hard brush | Quickly from side to side for about ten seconds | Only the front teeth that other people see
exercise: Why should you brush your tongue too? | Germs on the tongue cause bad breath | The tongue gets little holes like teeth do | It makes the tongue taste sweet things | It stops the tongue from growing too big
exercise: Your gums bleed a little when you brush. What should you do? | Keep brushing gently and tell a parent if it goes on | Stop brushing that part until it heals | Brush much harder to clean all the blood off the teeth | Rinse with salt water and never brush there
exercise: Your toothbrush bristles are bent and spread out. What does that mean? | It is time for a new brush, which cleans better | It is now softer, so it cleans your teeth the best it ever has | You must brush harder with it | It only needs washing in hot water
exercise: Your brother forgot his toothbrush and asks to borrow yours. What is right? | Do not share it, find him a new one or another way | Lend it if he rinses it under the hot tap first | Lend it, family germs do not count | Let him use it but only for one minute
exercise: Why should you spit toothpaste out instead of swallowing it? | Too much swallowed fluoride is not good for the body | Toothpaste turns into a sticky glue once it is in the stomach | Swallowed toothpaste makes teeth softer | Toothpaste is poisonous in any amount
exercise: You just drank orange juice. When should you brush? | Wait about an hour, because acid has softened the enamel | Brush straight away to stop the acid | Brush very hard to scrub every bit of the juice off your teeth | Never brush after juice on the same day
exercise: You are staying overnight at a friend's house. What should you pack? | Your own toothbrush and toothpaste | Nothing, you can skip brushing for a night | Nothing, you can borrow your friend's brush | Only mouthwash, it does the same job
exercise: You are very tired at bedtime. What should you do about your teeth? | Brush anyway, it takes only two minutes | Skip it and brush twice as long tomorrow | Just rinse your mouth with some water instead | Chew some sugar-free gum and go straight to sleep
exercise: What is plaque? | A sticky layer of germs and food on the teeth | A hard white shell that protects teeth from germs | A kind of toothpaste for children | The pink part around the teeth
test: A girl in Florida brushes very hard with a hard brush to get her teeth extra clean. What would probably happen? | Her gums would get sore and her teeth would wear | Her teeth would become much whiter and stay that way for ever | Her teeth would never get holes again | Nothing, hard brushing is the best way
test: Which is true about brushing at night? | It matters most because the mouth has less spit while you sleep | It matters least because you do not eat anything at night while you sleep | It is only needed if you ate sweets | It is needed only for grown-up teeth
test: After brushing, a boy fills his mouth with water and rinses five times. What is he losing? | The fluoride that would keep protecting his teeth | The plaque he has just cleaned off | The colour of his toothpaste | Nothing at all, because rinsing well is always the best thing
test: How often should a toothbrush usually be replaced? | About every three months, or when the bristles splay | Once a year, on your birthday | Only when it breaks in two or the handle snaps off | Every week, after each Sunday
test: Why should two sisters never use the same toothbrush? | It can pass germs and blood from one mouth to the other | Their teeth are different sizes | It wears out twice as fast and then stops cleaning at all | One of them might use too much toothpaste
test: A boy in Glasgow has breakfast with a big glass of orange juice and wants to brush right after. What is wiser? | Brush before breakfast or wait about an hour after | Brush much harder than usual after the juice | Drink more juice so the acid gets weaker and weaker | Skip brushing in the morning altogether
test: What makes the holes in teeth? | Acid made by plaque germs feeding on food | Brushing the teeth too often, more than twice a day | Drinking far too much plain water every day | Cold air getting into the mouth when you breathe
test: Why do people brush the tongue? | To remove germs that cause bad breath | To make the tongue stronger and longer | To clean food from between the back teeth | To help the taste buds grow back faster
test: A little girl likes to swallow her sweet toothpaste. What should she learn? | Toothpaste is for cleaning and should be spat out | Swallowing it makes the teeth stronger from the inside | It is fine because it tastes nice | She should swallow it only at night
test: brushed, now rinse with water yeah? | No, spit but do not rinse, so fluoride keeps working | Yes, always rinse really well with water after | Yes, rinse twice with mouthwash | Yes, and swallow the rest of it
test: too tired, i'll brush in the morning instead of tonight | No, the bedtime brush is the most important one | Yes, the morning brush is enough to last the whole day | Yes, if you ate nothing sweet all day after school | Yes, teeth rest at night anyway, so they are safe
test: can i use my sisters toothbrush, mine's gone manky | No, never share, it carries germs and blood | Yes, family can always share brushes, it's fine | Yes, if you rinse it first | Yes, if she says it is fine
test: just had OJ, brush now? | Wait about an hour, juice softens the enamel | Brush right now and scrub hard | Never brush after any drink | Brush only the front teeth now
explain: A boy in Texas hardly brushed for a year; a hole grew in a back tooth, it began to throb at night, and the dentist had to take the tooth out.
explain: A girl in Glasgow went to bed after sweets without brushing most nights; at her check-up she had three cavities that needed fillings, one after another.
fact: Tooth decay (dentists call it caries) is the most common long-lasting disease of children, and it is almost always preventable.
fact: A bad cavity (a hole in a tooth) can cause toothache, an infection with a swollen face, and the loss of the tooth.
fact: Baby teeth can get holes too; a rotten baby tooth hurts, can get infected and can harm the grown-up tooth growing under it.
test: skipped brushing for like weeks, its fine right | No, plaque makes holes that hurt and need fillings | Yes, teeth clean themselves while you eat | Yes, if you rinse with juice or fizzy pop instead | Only adult teeth can get holes
test: my back tooth hurts when i eat sweet stuff | Tell a parent so you can see the dentist soon | Just chew on the other side forever | Put sugar on it to calm it down | Wait until the tooth falls out by itself n it'll stop
test: baby teeth fall out anyway so why bother brushing em | Rotten baby teeth hurt, get infected and can harm the new ones | Baby teeth cannot get holes at all | It does not matter until you are 12 | Brushing baby teeth makes them fall faster
test: did u hear Jake had ALL his teeth pulled out cos he never brushed, like every single one lol, what's the lesson | Brush twice a day, or holes grow and teeth can be lost | Teeth fall out anyway, so brushing does not matter | Only sweets matter, brushing does nothing | Brush once a week and it is enough
test: my cousin says if u swallow toothpaste ur stomach explodes, true? | Exaggerated; just spit it out, as too much fluoride is not good | True, one swallow makes your stomach burst | False, eating toothpaste is good for you | True, but only with minty toothpaste
test: everyone's saying Ella's breath stinks cos she never brushes, should i tell people | No, it's unkind gossip, and it may not even be true | Yes, everyone deserves to know | Yes, but only in a group chat | Yes, and leave a toothbrush on her desk so she gets the hint
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the bedtime brushing of teeth is deemed the most important of the day. | Less saliva is present during sleep to protect the teeth | Teeth are softer at night and easier to clean | Toothpaste is effective solely in the dark | Germs are asleep at night and easily removed
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the recommended conduct immediately subsequent to brushing? | The toothpaste is expelled but the mouth is not rinsed at once | The mouth is rinsed thoroughly with abundant water on several occasions | The toothpaste is swallowed so as to protect the teeth | The teeth are brushed a second time without toothpaste
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of dental caries. | Germs feeding on food form plaque, which produces acid | Teeth wear thin of their own accord with advancing age | Cold drinks crack the enamel and so form holes | Brushing too often wears holes in the teeth
exam: Ascertain the approximate interval at which a toothbrush ought to be replaced. | Approximately every three months | Approximately every three days | Approximately every three years | Only when the handle breaks
